      "findings": [
        {
          "finding": "Population conditioning is the clearest fidelity candidate in this matched experiment.",
          "evidence": "All eight population/capture conditions have full scorable evaluation coverage (21 questions times 3 replicates). Sonnet 4.6 natural conditional parity gain is +0.0686443, paired question-level 95% BCa CI [0.0356307, 0.1147264]. Haiku natural distributional fidelity gain is +0.1492103, CI [0.0498643, 0.2682218], while its parity CI crosses zero. Development conditioning effects are positive for both models.",
          "candidate_decision": "Prioritize population-aware experimental routes and a larger question-level confirmation; do not change product defaults from this campaign alone."
        },
        {
          "finding": "Structured capture improves format reliability without demonstrating a parity gain.",
          "evidence": "All four structured population conditions have zero evaluation parse failures; natural conditioned Haiku and Sonnet 4.6 have 0.6878% and 2.0635% parse failures. No matched structured-minus-natural evaluation parity CI excludes zero in the positive direction. Haiku conditioned p_dist decreases by 0.0615115, CI [-0.1454287, -0.0170687].",
          "candidate_decision": "Expose structured capture for explicit format needs; do not advertise it as a fidelity upgrade. Keep Sonnet 5.5 forced structured capture unsupported."
        },
        {
          "finding": "Increasing samples from 10 to 150 does not establish an evaluation parity improvement.",
          "evidence": "On 21 complete/scorable evaluation questions, Haiku parity delta is +0.0140322, CI [-0.0044621,0.0392097]; Sonnet 5.5 delta is -0.00041855, CI [-0.0055430,0.0060635]. All 10/20/30/60/100/150 prefixes are preserved. Haiku p_dist improves modestly; Sonnet p_dist decreases modestly.",
          "candidate_decision": "Prefer broad question coverage over automatically increasing synthetic samples to 150; test adaptive sample counts separately."
        },
        {
          "finding": "Provider/request path matters and blanket model rankings are unsupported.",
          "evidence": "Opus Althing-minus-raw evaluation parity is +0.137998 on 16 GlobalOpinionQA questions, CI [0.0648045,0.2394969], and +0.191001 on 18 population-average SubPOP questions, CI [0.0848849,0.2693029]. Overall 13 fully captured raw-provider cells remain unscorable, so affected paired contrasts are withheld. The natural conditioned population comparison costs $0.741321 accounted for 5,400 Haiku samples versus $15.084479 for the same Sonnet 4.6 sample count; the model parity difference CI crosses zero, which is not an equivalence result.",
          "candidate_decision": "Keep provider/capture identity explicit. Consider Haiku as a cost-efficient research route, with broader confirmation before selecting a fidelity default."
        }
      ]
    },
